  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What are plastic pellets?",
      "a": "Pellets are small beads of plastic, usually made from oil or gas. The plastic is a polymer: very long chain molecules built from many small ones joined together. Pellets are easy to store, pour and melt evenly.",
      "asks": [
        "What is plastic made from?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What does the screw do in injection moulding?",
      "a": "The screw turns to carry pellets forward, mixes them and helps melt them by friction. Then it moves forward like a syringe plunger to inject the melted plastic into the mould.",
      "asks": [
        "How is plastic melted in a moulding machine?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why must the mould be clamped so hard?",
      "a": "The molten plastic is injected at very high pressure, which tries to force the mould halves apart. Big machines clamp with hundreds or thousands of tonnes of force to keep the joint sealed.",
      "asks": [
        "What does the clamp do?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How is the mould made?",
      "a": "Moulds are carved from hardened steel or aluminium by CNC milling and spark erosion, often with several cavities to make many parts at once. They are expensive, so moulding pays off when you need thousands of parts.",
      "asks": [
        "Why is injection moulding only used for big batches?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does the part need cooling before it comes out?",
      "a": "The plastic must become solid enough to hold its shape when pushed out. Cooling channels carry water through the mould, and the cooling time is often the longest part of each cycle.",
      "asks": [
        "How long does injection moulding take?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "John Wesley Hyatt and his brother Isaiah patented an early injection moulding machine in 1872.",
    "Injection moulding cycles typically last from a few seconds to about a minute."
  ],
